schism
SCHISM, s. शाखा, शाखाभेदः, धर्म्मभेदः, मतभेदः, मार्गभेदः, सम्प्रदायः<BR>धर्म्मविभेदः, मतविभेदः, धर्म्मविच्छेदः.<BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslp?dict=MWE&page=713-b" target="_blank">PDF</a><BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslc?dict=mwe" target="_blank">Correction</a>

schismatic
SCHISMATIC, SCHISMATICAL, a. धर्म्मभेदकः -का -कं, मतभेदकः &c., मार्ग-<BR>भेदकः &c., शाखाभेदसम्बन्धी -न्धिनी -न्धि (न्), मार्गभेदसम्बन्धी &c.,<BR>मार्गभेदविपयकः -का -कं.<BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslp?dict=MWE&page=713-b" target="_blank">PDF</a><BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslc?dict=mwe" target="_blank">Correction</a>

scholar
SCHOLAR, s. (Learner, pupil) शिष्यः, विद्यार्थी m. (न्), अन्तेवासी m.,<BR>छात्रः, अन्तेवासिजनः, अध्यायी m., अध्येता m. (तृ), विद्याभ्यासी m.<BR>(न्), शिक्षिताक्षरः, अधीती m., विद्याभ्यासकृत् m., अक्षरमुखः, पाठार्थी m.;<BR>‘head scholar,’ पट्टशिष्यः; ‘scholar of a scholar,’ प्रशिष्यः.<BR>—<BR>(Learned man) पण्डितः, पण्डितजनः, विद्वान् m. (स्), विद्वज्जनः,<BR>कृतविद्यः, सर्व्वरसः, रसज्ञः, तान्त्रिकः, see LEARNED.<BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslp?dict=MWE&page=713-b" target="_blank">PDF</a><BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslc?dict=mwe" target="_blank">Correction</a>

scholar-like
SCHOLAR-LIKE, SCHOLARLY, a. पण्डितयोग्यः -ग्या -ग्यं, छात्रयोग्यः &c.<BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslp?dict=MWE&page=713-b" target="_blank">PDF</a><BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslc?dict=mwe" target="_blank">Correction</a>

scholarship
SCHOLARSHIP, s. पाण्डित्पं, पण्डितत्वं, विद्या, वैदुष्यं, see LEARNING.<BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslp?dict=MWE&page=713-b" target="_blank">PDF</a><BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslc?dict=mwe" target="_blank">Correction</a>

scholastic
SCHOLASTIC, SCHOLASTICAL, a. पाठशालासम्बन्धी -न्धिनी -न्धि(न्),<BR>शालासम्बन्धी &c., पण्डितसभासम्बन्धी &c., पाण्डित्यविषयकः -का -कं.<BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslp?dict=MWE&page=713-b" target="_blank">PDF</a><BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslc?dict=mwe" target="_blank">Correction</a>

scholiast
SCHOLIAST, s. भाप्यकारः, टीकाकारः, टीकालेखकः, see COMMENTATOR.<BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslp?dict=MWE&page=713-b" target="_blank">PDF</a><BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslc?dict=mwe" target="_blank">Correction</a>

scholium
SCHOLIUM, s. टीका, भाप्यं, टीकाग्रन्थः, आदर्शः, see COMMENTARY.<BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslp?dict=MWE&page=713-b" target="_blank">PDF</a><BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslc?dict=mwe" target="_blank">Correction</a>

school
SCHOOL, s., (Place of instruction) पाठशाला, विद्यालयः -यं, विद्या-<BR>वेश्म n. (न्), विद्यागृहं, शिक्षालयः -यं, शिक्षागृहं, विद्याभ्यासगृहं, वि-<BR>द्याभ्यासशाला, अध्ययनशाला, अध्ययनगृहं, मठः, आश्रमः, अवसथः,<BR>अवसथ्यः, शाला.<BR>— (Body of scholars) शिष्यसमूहः, विद्यार्थिसमूहः,<BR>छात्रसमूहः, पाठशाला, शाला.<BR>— (Sect of philosophers) शाखा,<BR>पण्डितशाखा, शिक्षासम्प्रदायः, शालासम्प्रदायः; ‘difference of school,’<BR>शाखाभेदः; ‘of the same school,’ एकशाखः -खा -खं; ‘of a<BR>good school,’ शालाशुद्धः -द्धा -द्धं.<BR>— (School or system of phi-<BR>losophy) दर्शनः, तीर्थः. There are six schools or systems<BR>of philosophy recognised by the Hindus, viz. 1. Pūrva Mī-<BR>[<a href="https://dub.sh/cslp?dict=MWE&page=714-a" target="_blank">Page714-a+ 51</a>]<BR>mānsā, पूर्व्वमीमांसा, founded by Jaimini, for the purpose of<BR>illustrating and explaining the karmakānda, or ritual part<BR>of the Vedas. It gives the rationale or meaning of the<BR>ritual enjoined in those sacred books, as distinct from<BR>the theological or spiritual part of them, and acknow-<BR>ledges the principle of एकत्वं, or oneness, which it was<BR>the object of the Vedānta system more fully to investi-<BR>gate. 2. Sānkhya, सांख्यः, ascribed to the Muni Kapila.<BR>This system is an attempt to account for the existence<BR>of the universe without the Deity, by asserting the<BR>principle of द्वैतं or duality, or, in other words, the co-ex-<BR>istence of Spirit and matter. 3. Vedānta, वेदान्तः, or<BR>Uttara Mīmānsā, उत्तरमीमांसा, said to have been founded by<BR>Vyāsa, and subsequently fully developed by its most<BR>celebrated teacher शङ्करः or शङ्कराचार्य्यः. This system is<BR>wholly concerned with the theological and philosophical<BR>part of the Vedas, or that part contained in the chapters<BR>called Upanishads, and its teaching is intended to oppose<BR>the duality of the Sānkhya system by shewing that the<BR>end or aim of the Vedas is एकत्वं, or the oneness of<BR>Spirit. It denies the co-existence of matter with Spirit,<BR>and hence^1 the conclusion arrived at is the non-existence<BR>of any thing but God. 4. Nyāya, न्यायः, founded by the<BR>inspired sage Gautama, and the most popular of all<BR>the systems. It is neither so mystical as the Vedānta,<BR>nor so fanciful as the Sānkhya. Its title signifies ‘the<BR>fitness of things,’ and it may be described as an attempt<BR>to treat of almost all subjects, according to a philosophical<BR>method and logical arrangement. 5. Vaiśeṣika, वैशेषिकं,<BR>a branch of the Nyāya, founded by the sage कणादः, to<BR>supply what was incomplete in the aphorisms of Gautama.<BR>6. Yoga, योगः or Pātanjala, पातञ्जलं, founded by the sage<BR>पतञ्जलिः m. Its object is to shew that, even in the body,<BR>there may be, to a great extent, union with the Supreme<BR>Being. These philosophical systems are contained in<BR>sacred writings called Ṣāstras. See the word.<BR>[1] Correction submitted by Dr. Sampada Savardekar on 20210422. Reference: https://github.com/sanskrit-lexicon/csl-corrections/issues/61.<BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslp?dict=MWE&page=713-b" target="_blank">PDF</a><BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslc?dict=mwe" target="_blank">Correction</a>

school
