syllogism
SYLLOGISM, s. न्यायः, अवयवी m. (न्), अवयवघटितवाक्यं, अवयवयुक्त-<BR>वाक्यं, अवयवोपेतवाक्यं, आनुपूर्व्वं. A syllogism has five mem-<BR>bers or अवयवाः, according to the Nyāya system, viz.<BR>1. The proposition or assertion to be proved, प्रतिज्ञा; 2. the<BR>reason given, हेतुः; 3. the example or illustration, उदाहरणं;<BR>4. the application, उपनयः; 5. the conclusion, निगमनं. Thus,<BR>1. ‘The hill is fiery,’ पर्व्वतो वह्निमान्. 2. ‘Because it smokes,’<BR>धूमवत्त्वात्. 3. ‘Whatever smokes is fiery, like a culinary<BR>hearth,’ यो यो धूमवान् स वह्निमान् यथा महानसः. 4. ‘This (hill)<BR>has smoke which is always accompanied with fire,’ वह्नि-<BR>व्याप्यधूमवान् अयं. 5. ‘Therefore it is fiery,’ तस्माद् वह्निमान्.<BR>According to the Mīmānsā school, a syllogism has only<BR>the last three members.<BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslp?dict=MWE&page=793-a" target="_blank">PDF</a><BR><a href="https://dub.sh/cslc?dict=mwe" target="_blank">Correction</a>

syllogistic
